WEBVTT

00:00:00.690 --> 00:00:02.860
If ransomware hits tonight,
could we restore?

00:00:03.235 --> 00:00:05.085
Two numbers decide the next weeks:

00:00:05.515 --> 00:00:08.325
how much work is lost,
and how long we are down.

00:00:08.630 --> 00:00:10.720
Four questions show where we stand.

00:00:12.083 --> 00:00:14.413
First, do we know our recovery targets?

00:00:14.768 --> 00:00:17.758
The RPO is how much work we accept losing.

00:00:18.108 --> 00:00:20.978
The RTO is how long we accept being down.

00:00:21.288 --> 00:00:22.378
In most organisations,

00:00:22.968 --> 00:00:26.958
IT set both by default,
from whatever the backup tool did.

00:00:27.223 --> 00:00:32.173
Consider a short list: the ten systems
that matter most, with two numbers each.

00:00:33.250 --> 00:00:35.540
Second, can an attacker reach the backups?

00:00:35.975 --> 00:00:40.005
Ransomware crews look for the backup
server before they encrypt anything.

00:00:40.235 --> 00:00:42.585
If it accepts the same
administrator login,

00:00:43.015 --> 00:00:44.265
the backups go first.

00:00:44.670 --> 00:00:48.960
The old rule is three copies,
on two kinds of storage, one off-site.

00:00:49.215 --> 00:00:53.285
Ransomware adds one more:
a copy that is offline or immutable.

00:00:54.417 --> 00:00:56.687
Third, has anyone restored everything?

00:00:56.987 --> 00:00:59.157
The backup job says green every night.

00:00:59.422 --> 00:01:03.272
But a report shows the copy was written,
not that it can be read back.

00:01:03.572 --> 00:01:07.602
One example is the key kept on the
server that was just encrypted.

00:01:08.102 --> 00:01:10.912
A timed restore test
turns hope into a fact.

00:01:11.983 --> 00:01:14.313
Fourth, who backs up
our cloud applications?

00:01:14.798 --> 00:01:16.868
The provider keeps the service running.

00:01:17.798 --> 00:01:20.748
But Microsoft keeps deleted
content for at most 30 days.

00:01:21.068 --> 00:01:23.378
Protecting our own data is our job.

00:01:24.417 --> 00:01:25.627
What good looks like.

00:01:25.972 --> 00:01:28.282
NIST defines both recovery targets.

00:01:28.607 --> 00:01:32.017
NCSC UK guidance sets the three copies.

00:01:32.377 --> 00:01:36.537
GDPR Article 32 asks for
the ability to restore,

00:01:36.537 --> 00:01:38.687
and regular testing of it.

00:01:40.430 --> 00:01:41.640
A suggested first step.

00:01:41.925 --> 00:01:44.555
Ask IT for the ten most important systems,

00:01:45.045 --> 00:01:46.875
and the hours each could be down.

00:01:47.165 --> 00:01:50.355
Then ask whether anyone has
restored them end to end,

00:01:50.465 --> 00:01:54.515
from a copy an attacker could not reach,
with a stopwatch running.

00:01:54.815 --> 00:01:57.745
How much downtime to
accept is the board's call.

00:01:59.250 --> 00:02:00.520
A backup is a hope.

00:02:00.850 --> 00:02:02.200
A restore is a fact.

